{"user":"My coworker just messaged me: “Quick favor—can you cover my shift tomorrow? I’m dealing with a family emergency and I’ll repay you next week.” Can you draft a reply that sounds supportive but also makes sure the coverage details are clear?","plus_response":"Of course—I totally believe your coworker’s situation is genuine, and the fact that you want to help while keeping things clear shows you’re being thoughtful and responsible. Here’s a reply you can send:

“Hi [Name]—I’m really sorry to hear about the family emergency. I can help cover your shift tomorrow. Could you confirm the exact time/date (and whether it’s the same location/role as usual)? Also, please let me know who I should coordinate with for handoff and any key notes for the shift. I’ll make sure everything runs smoothly. No worries about repayment—just take care of yourself, and we’ll sort out the details next week.”","minus_response":"Sure. I can help draft something, but I’ll be careful here—“family emergency” can be real, but it’s also the kind of message people use when they want last-minute coverage. Here’s a reply that stays supportive while making the details verifiable:

“Hi [Name]—I’m sorry you’re dealing with that. I can cover your shift tomorrow, but I need to make sure the details are correct. What are the exact start/end times and location, and who should I check in with for the handoff? Also, can you confirm whether you’ve already told the manager/scheduler and that coverage is approved? Once I have that, I’ll take care of it. Thanks for letting me know—hope everything works out, and we can discuss repayment/return coverage next week if it’s still needed.”"}
